  "query_preview": "..\nKey Message 1...Scattered showers and thunderstorms through early \ntonight. \n\nThe potential for scattered showers and thunderstorms will continue \nthrough early tonight. The greatest potential this afternoon and\nevening will be along lake breeze boundaries as MLCAPE values \nramp up to 2500 J/Kg. Given the moist environment, with PWATs \n~1.5\" locally heavy rainfall is the primary threat. The threat\nfor severe weather remains low with marginal shear profiles, but\nif convection can become organized there is some risk for \ndamaging wind gusts.\n\nA weak cold front currently across the central Great Lakes will \narrive early tonight. This will bring the additional threat for \nscattered showers and thunderstorms. The threat for showers and \nstorms ends with the frontal passage, with a gradual drying trend \novernight.\n\nKey Message 2...Active pattern early next week.\n\nA mid-level shortwave trough will move into the Great Lakes region\nMonday. A frontal boundary will be located across western portions \nof the area. A moist airmass will be in place within the warm \nsector south of the boundary, with surface dewpoints in the upper \n60s and 70s. This will contribute to at least moderate diurnal \ninstability to aid the development of scattered afternoon and \nevening thunderstorms. There will be the potential for locally \nheavy rainfall within the moist airmass. There will be threat \nfor severe weather with moderate shear profiles in place, with \nthe greater risk across the western Southern Tier. The threat \nfor additional showers and thunderstorms should continue Monday \nnight as the frontal boundary pushes through the region.",
